1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,830
Imagine you run the card team at a small bank.

2
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:05,709
Every day, ten thousand card payments go through.

3
00:00:05,709 --> 00:00:08,540
And about one in every hundred of them is fraud.

4
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:11,000
Two companies want to sell you a fraud detector.

5
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:14,720
The first one says: our model catches ninety percent of fraud.

6
00:00:15,070 --> 00:00:18,445
The second one says: ninety percent of our alarms are real

7
00:00:18,445 --> 00:00:18,840
fraud.

8
00:00:19,190 --> 00:00:22,296
And both of them add: by the way, we are more than ninety-nine

9
00:00:22,296 --> 00:00:23,300
percent accurate.

10
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:25,209
They sound like the same promise.

11
00:00:25,209 --> 00:00:27,140
Ninety, ninety, ninety-nine.

12
00:00:27,490 --> 00:00:30,527
But they are completely different promises.

13
00:00:30,527 --> 00:00:33,545
One of these detectors would let more than half of the fraud

14
00:00:33,545 --> 00:00:34,570
walk straight past you.

15
00:00:35,570 --> 00:00:37,355
And here's the strangest part.

16
00:00:37,355 --> 00:00:41,374
A detector that does nothing at all, that never raises a single

17
00:00:41,374 --> 00:00:44,720
alarm, is ninety-nine percent accurate too.

18
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:49,127
At the end of our prosecutor's fallacy video,

19
00:00:49,127 --> 00:00:51,053
I left you with this exact puzzle.

20
00:00:51,053 --> 00:00:52,560
Today, we solve it.

21
00:00:52,910 --> 00:00:55,777
With one small table, two by two, that tells you the whole truth

22
00:00:55,777 --> 00:00:57,730
about any yes or no prediction.

23
00:00:58,080 --> 00:00:59,690
It's called the confusion matrix.

24
00:01:00,690 --> 00:01:02,242
Welcome to Math Lab TV.

25
00:01:02,242 --> 00:01:04,460
Where math is something you can see.

26
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:09,100
Today you will learn the four boxes of the confusion matrix,

27
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:11,180
and how to fill them from real counts.

28
00:01:11,530 --> 00:01:15,334
You will see why accuracy can lie, and what precision and recall

29
00:01:15,334 --> 00:01:16,350
tell you instead.

30
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:20,097
And you will practise on four real examples,

31
00:01:20,097 --> 00:01:24,360
until you can read any of these tables on your own.

32
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:27,915
Let's start with that lazy detector, because it shows the whole

33
00:01:27,915 --> 00:01:28,500
problem.

34
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:32,205
Its rule is simple. Whatever the payment is,

35
00:01:32,205 --> 00:01:33,440
say: not fraud.

36
00:01:33,790 --> 00:01:36,782
Out of ten thousand payments, nine thousand nine hundred really

37
00:01:36,782 --> 00:01:39,070
are fine. So it gets all of those right.

38
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:41,864
The one hundred frauds, it gets wrong.

39
00:01:41,864 --> 00:01:43,420
Every single one.

40
00:01:43,770 --> 00:01:46,375
Nine thousand nine hundred right, out of ten thousand.

41
00:01:46,375 --> 00:01:48,580
That's ninety-nine percent accuracy.

42
00:01:49,380 --> 00:01:51,040
And it caught zero fraud.

43
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:51,980
Not one.

44
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:56,893
So accuracy, on its own, can't tell a useless detector from a

45
00:01:56,893 --> 00:01:57,630
good one.

46
00:01:57,980 --> 00:02:01,680
This happens whenever the thing you are looking for is rare.

47
00:02:02,030 --> 00:02:04,897
Fraud is rare. Serious diseases are rare.

48
00:02:04,897 --> 00:02:07,764
Faulty parts on a factory line are rare.

49
00:02:07,764 --> 00:02:10,190
Hackers in your network logs are rare.

50
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:13,591
And those are exactly the cases where getting it wrong costs the

51
00:02:13,591 --> 00:02:13,990
most.

52
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:17,476
In our Bayes video, we saw a test that was ninety percent

53
00:02:17,476 --> 00:02:21,159
accurate, where a positive result meant only about a one in

54
00:02:21,159 --> 00:02:22,290
twelve chance of being sick.

55
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:25,988
The confusion matrix is the tool that keeps all of those numbers

56
00:02:25,988 --> 00:02:28,520
in one place, so nothing gets hidden.

57
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:31,391
Every time a detector looks at a payment,

58
00:02:31,391 --> 00:02:32,400
two things are true.

59
00:02:32,750 --> 00:02:34,987
First, what actually happened.

60
00:02:34,987 --> 00:02:37,000
Was it really fraud, or not?

61
00:02:37,350 --> 00:02:39,588
Second, what the detector said.

62
00:02:39,588 --> 00:02:42,050
Did it raise an alarm, or stay quiet?

63
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:44,355
Two questions, two answers each.

64
00:02:44,355 --> 00:02:46,524
That gives four possible outcomes.

65
00:02:46,524 --> 00:02:47,830
No more, no less.

66
00:02:48,430 --> 00:02:50,168
Let's lay them out in a grid.

67
00:02:50,168 --> 00:02:51,690
The rows are the truth.

68
00:02:51,690 --> 00:02:53,212
The top row is real fraud.

69
00:02:53,212 --> 00:02:54,950
The bottom row is not fraud.

70
00:02:55,300 --> 00:02:58,142
The columns are what the detector said.

71
00:02:58,142 --> 00:03:00,250
The left column is: alarm.

72
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:02,120
The right column is: quiet.

73
00:03:02,470 --> 00:03:05,864
Now let's name each box carefully, because the names trip

74
00:03:05,864 --> 00:03:06,930
everyone up.

75
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:11,557
Top left. It really was fraud, and the detector raised an alarm.

76
00:03:11,557 --> 00:03:14,550
A correct catch. We call it a true positive.

77
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:18,361
Positive means the detector said yes, this is the thing.

78
00:03:18,361 --> 00:03:19,570
True means it was right.

79
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:23,637
Bottom right. It was a normal payment, and the detector stayed

80
00:03:23,637 --> 00:03:26,550
quiet. Also correct. That's a true negative.

81
00:03:27,150 --> 00:03:29,207
Now the mistakes. Bottom left.

82
00:03:29,207 --> 00:03:33,064
A perfectly normal payment, but the detector raised an alarm

83
00:03:33,064 --> 00:03:33,890
anyway.

84
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:37,025
A false alarm. We call it a false positive.

85
00:03:37,025 --> 00:03:39,420
The detector said yes, and it was wrong.

86
00:03:40,020 --> 00:03:43,403
And top right. Real fraud, and the detector said nothing.

87
00:03:43,403 --> 00:03:44,670
The fraud slipped through.

88
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:47,655
A miss. We call it a false negative.

89
00:03:47,655 --> 00:03:50,290
The detector said no, and it was wrong.

90
00:03:50,890 --> 00:03:52,984
Here's a trick to remember them.

91
00:03:52,984 --> 00:03:55,496
The second word is what the detector said.

92
00:03:55,496 --> 00:03:57,590
The first word is whether that was right.

93
00:03:58,590 --> 00:04:01,344
So a false negative is: it said negative,

94
00:04:01,344 --> 00:04:04,510
and that was false. It said no, but the answer was yes.

95
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:07,280
Let's try it slowly with just ten payments,

96
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:09,480
before we use the big numbers.

97
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:11,020
Three of them are fraud.

98
00:04:11,370 --> 00:04:15,034
Payment one is fraud, and the detector raises an alarm.

99
00:04:15,034 --> 00:04:16,683
Fraud row, alarm column.

100
00:04:16,683 --> 00:04:17,910
A true positive.

101
00:04:18,260 --> 00:04:21,940
Payment two is a normal coffee, and the detector stays quiet.

102
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:23,460
Fine row, quiet column.

103
00:04:23,460 --> 00:04:24,760
A true negative.

104
00:04:25,110 --> 00:04:29,493
Payment three is a normal payment, but it's a big one in another

105
00:04:29,493 --> 00:04:31,905
country, so the detector panics.

106
00:04:31,905 --> 00:04:33,220
A false positive.

107
00:04:33,570 --> 00:04:37,583
Payment four is fraud, but a small one, and it slips through.

108
00:04:37,583 --> 00:04:39,000
A false negative.

109
00:04:39,350 --> 00:04:43,650
We keep going, one payment at a time, until all ten are sorted.

110
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:49,126
Two true positives, one false negative, two false positives,

111
00:04:49,126 --> 00:04:51,082
and five true negatives.

112
00:04:51,082 --> 00:04:53,783
Two plus one plus two plus five is ten.

113
00:04:53,783 --> 00:04:56,980
Every payment has exactly one home.

114
00:04:57,980 --> 00:05:01,293
The two correct boxes sit on one diagonal.

115
00:05:01,293 --> 00:05:04,130
The two kinds of mistake sit on the other.

116
00:05:05,130 --> 00:05:07,007
Now let's fill this grid with Detector A,

117
00:05:07,007 --> 00:05:09,380
the one that catches ninety percent of fraud.

118
00:05:09,730 --> 00:05:12,677
We run it on our ten thousand payments, and sort every payment

119
00:05:12,677 --> 00:05:14,340
into one of the four boxes.

120
00:05:14,690 --> 00:05:16,678
Start with the one hundred real frauds.

121
00:05:16,678 --> 00:05:18,470
They all belong in the top row.

122
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:21,968
Detector A raises an alarm on ninety of them.

123
00:05:21,968 --> 00:05:23,750
Ninety true positives.

124
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:25,550
The other ten get through.

125
00:05:25,550 --> 00:05:27,000
Ten false negatives.

126
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:30,670
Now the nine thousand nine hundred normal payments.

127
00:05:30,670 --> 00:05:32,790
They belong in the bottom row.

128
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:35,890
Detector A wrongly flags thirty of them.

129
00:05:35,890 --> 00:05:37,720
Thirty false positives.

130
00:05:37,720 --> 00:05:41,390
Thirty customers who get a worried phone call for nothing.

131
00:05:41,740 --> 00:05:45,067
And the remaining nine thousand eight hundred and seventy are

132
00:05:45,067 --> 00:05:47,660
correctly left alone. True negatives.

133
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:51,877
Let's check our work. Ninety plus ten is one hundred.

134
00:05:51,877 --> 00:05:53,160
That's every fraud.

135
00:05:53,510 --> 00:05:56,580
Thirty plus nine thousand eight hundred and seventy is nine

136
00:05:56,580 --> 00:05:59,680
thousand nine hundred. That's every normal payment.

137
00:06:00,030 --> 00:06:02,784
And all four boxes together make ten thousand.

138
00:06:02,784 --> 00:06:05,750
Every payment lands in exactly one box.

139
00:06:06,350 --> 00:06:07,892
Now add up the left column.

140
00:06:07,892 --> 00:06:10,755
Ninety plus thirty is one hundred and twenty.

141
00:06:10,755 --> 00:06:13,240
That's every alarm Detector A raised.

142
00:06:13,590 --> 00:06:15,251
So the rows tell you the truth.

143
00:06:15,251 --> 00:06:17,736
The columns tell you what the detector did.

144
00:06:17,736 --> 00:06:21,661
Hold on to that, because every number in this video comes from

145
00:06:21,661 --> 00:06:24,160
reading this grid by row, or by column.

146
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:27,243
Let's start with the number the salespeople love.

147
00:06:27,243 --> 00:06:28,140
Accuracy.

148
00:06:28,490 --> 00:06:31,942
Accuracy asks one question: out of everything,

149
00:06:31,942 --> 00:06:33,970
how often was the detector right?

150
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:35,976
Right means the diagonal.

151
00:06:35,976 --> 00:06:38,830
True positives plus true negatives.

152
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:41,080
Then divide by everything.

153
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:42,140
All four boxes.

154
00:06:42,490 --> 00:06:46,071
In symbols, accuracy equals T P plus T N,

155
00:06:46,071 --> 00:06:49,876
over the total. T P is short for true positives.

156
00:06:49,876 --> 00:06:52,340
T N is short for true negatives.

157
00:06:52,690 --> 00:06:56,195
For Detector A, that's ninety plus nine thousand eight hundred

158
00:06:56,195 --> 00:06:59,920
and seventy. Nine thousand nine hundred and sixty right answers.

159
00:07:00,270 --> 00:07:03,880
Out of ten thousand. That's ninety-nine point six percent.

160
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:07,651
Sounds amazing. But remember the lazy detector scored

161
00:07:07,651 --> 00:07:09,590
ninety-nine percent by doing nothing.

162
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:13,324
So all of Detector A's real work, catching ninety frauds,

163
00:07:13,324 --> 00:07:17,469
only moves accuracy from ninety-nine to ninety-nine point six.

164
00:07:17,469 --> 00:07:18,670
Less than one point.

165
00:07:19,670 --> 00:07:22,339
Why? Because the bottom right box is huge.

166
00:07:22,339 --> 00:07:25,233
Nine thousand eight hundred and seventy easy,

167
00:07:25,233 --> 00:07:28,570
boring, correct answers drown out everything else.

168
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:30,042
That's the rule of thumb.

169
00:07:30,042 --> 00:07:33,689
When one class is rare, accuracy is mostly a measure of how rare

170
00:07:33,689 --> 00:07:34,270
it is.

171
00:07:35,470 --> 00:07:37,526
Accuracy is not useless.

172
00:07:37,526 --> 00:07:39,990
When the two groups are roughly the same size,

173
00:07:39,990 --> 00:07:41,230
it's a fine first look.

174
00:07:41,230 --> 00:07:43,490
We'll see that in the spam example later.

175
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:46,709
But for rare things, we need questions that zoom in on the fraud

176
00:07:46,709 --> 00:07:49,420
itself. And there are exactly two of them.

177
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:52,390
The first question is about your alarms.

178
00:07:52,740 --> 00:07:56,719
Picture the analyst who has to phone every flagged customer.

179
00:07:56,719 --> 00:07:58,819
They only ever see the alarms.

180
00:07:58,819 --> 00:08:00,710
They never see the quiet payments.

181
00:08:01,060 --> 00:08:04,579
So their question is: when the detector raises an alarm,

182
00:08:04,579 --> 00:08:06,030
how often is it real?

183
00:08:06,380 --> 00:08:09,940
That's precision. And it lives entirely in the left column.

184
00:08:10,540 --> 00:08:14,377
Precision equals true positives, over all the alarms.

185
00:08:14,377 --> 00:08:16,728
T P, over T P plus F P.

186
00:08:16,728 --> 00:08:20,140
F P is short for false positives, the false alarms.

187
00:08:20,490 --> 00:08:24,428
For Detector A, the left column holds ninety real frauds and

188
00:08:24,428 --> 00:08:25,810
thirty false alarms.

189
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:29,004
Ninety, out of one hundred and twenty.

190
00:08:29,004 --> 00:08:30,827
That's three out of every four.

191
00:08:30,827 --> 00:08:32,850
Seventy-five percent precision.

192
00:08:33,850 --> 00:08:37,253
In plain words: every time Detector A rings the bell,

193
00:08:37,253 --> 00:08:39,590
there's a three in four chance it's real fraud,

194
00:08:39,590 --> 00:08:42,350
and a one in four chance it's a false alarm.

195
00:08:42,700 --> 00:08:44,909
Notice what precision ignores.

196
00:08:44,909 --> 00:08:47,561
It doesn't care about the right column at all.

197
00:08:47,561 --> 00:08:49,770
It doesn't know how much fraud got away.

198
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:53,563
A detector that raised one single alarm, and happened to be

199
00:08:53,563 --> 00:08:55,436
right, would have perfect precision.

200
00:08:55,436 --> 00:08:56,730
One out of one.

201
00:08:57,730 --> 00:09:00,587
So precision alone can be gamed by being very,

202
00:09:00,587 --> 00:09:01,560
very cautious.

203
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:03,944
That's why we need the second question.

204
00:09:03,944 --> 00:09:05,380
It's about what you missed.

205
00:09:05,730 --> 00:09:07,398
Picture the bank's fraud manager.

206
00:09:07,398 --> 00:09:09,789
They care about every fraud that happened,

207
00:09:09,789 --> 00:09:12,180
whether the detector noticed it or not.

208
00:09:12,530 --> 00:09:16,223
Their question is: out of all the real fraud,

209
00:09:16,223 --> 00:09:18,070
how much did we catch?

210
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:21,760
That's recall. And it lives entirely in the top row.

211
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:27,979
Recall equals true positives, over all the real positives.

212
00:09:27,979 --> 00:09:31,009
T P, over T P plus F N.

213
00:09:31,009 --> 00:09:35,190
F N is short for false negatives, the misses.

214
00:09:35,540 --> 00:09:39,400
For Detector A, the top row holds ninety catches and ten misses.

215
00:09:39,750 --> 00:09:41,624
Ninety, out of one hundred.

216
00:09:41,624 --> 00:09:42,960
Ninety percent recall.

217
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:45,951
That's the first salesperson's claim.

218
00:09:45,951 --> 00:09:48,163
We catch ninety percent of fraud.

219
00:09:48,163 --> 00:09:49,490
That's a recall claim.

220
00:09:50,490 --> 00:09:53,333
You'll also hear recall called sensitivity,

221
00:09:53,333 --> 00:09:54,966
or the true positive rate.

222
00:09:54,966 --> 00:09:57,020
Same number, different names.

223
00:09:57,370 --> 00:10:00,422
There's also a matching number for the bottom row,

224
00:10:00,422 --> 00:10:03,693
called specificity. Of all the normal payments,

225
00:10:03,693 --> 00:10:05,532
how many did we leave alone?

226
00:10:05,532 --> 00:10:09,190
For Detector A, nine thousand eight hundred and seventy out of

227
00:10:09,190 --> 00:10:10,624
nine thousand nine hundred.

228
00:10:10,624 --> 00:10:14,300
Almost all of them. We'll need it when we meet the R O C curve.

229
00:10:14,650 --> 00:10:16,347
And recall has its own blind spot.

230
00:10:16,347 --> 00:10:19,740
It ignores the bottom row, so it never sees false alarms.

231
00:10:20,090 --> 00:10:24,102
A detector that flags every single payment as fraud catches all

232
00:10:24,102 --> 00:10:27,610
one hundred frauds. One hundred percent recall.

233
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:30,215
And it phones all ten thousand customers.

234
00:10:30,215 --> 00:10:31,360
That's useless too.

235
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:34,247
So here's the picture to keep.

236
00:10:34,247 --> 00:10:36,550
Precision reads down the alarm column.

237
00:10:36,550 --> 00:10:38,437
Recall reads across the fraud row.

238
00:10:38,437 --> 00:10:42,418
They share one box, the true positives, and look away from each

239
00:10:42,418 --> 00:10:44,930
other in every other direction.

240
00:10:46,430 --> 00:10:48,383
Now we can finally answer the puzzle.

241
00:10:48,383 --> 00:10:51,550
Let's fill the grid for Detector B, the one whose alarms are

242
00:10:51,550 --> 00:10:52,810
ninety percent real.

243
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:55,169
Detector B is much more careful.

244
00:10:55,169 --> 00:10:57,000
It only raises fifty alarms.

245
00:10:57,350 --> 00:10:59,562
Forty-five of them are real fraud.

246
00:10:59,562 --> 00:11:03,545
Five are false alarms. So its precision is forty-five out of

247
00:11:03,545 --> 00:11:06,640
fifty. Ninety percent. Its claim was true.

248
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:09,135
But look at the top row.

249
00:11:09,135 --> 00:11:12,280
Forty-five caught. And fifty-five frauds missed.

250
00:11:12,630 --> 00:11:15,705
Its recall is forty-five out of one hundred.

251
00:11:15,705 --> 00:11:18,781
Forty-five percent. It lets more than half the fraud walk

252
00:11:18,781 --> 00:11:19,440
straight past.

253
00:11:20,640 --> 00:11:24,175
And its accuracy? Forty-five plus nine thousand eight hundred

254
00:11:24,175 --> 00:11:26,687
and ninety-five, out of ten thousand.

255
00:11:26,687 --> 00:11:28,564
Ninety-nine point four percent.

256
00:11:28,564 --> 00:11:29,820
Still sounds amazing.

257
00:11:30,620 --> 00:11:34,721
So side by side. Detector A: precision seventy-five,

258
00:11:34,721 --> 00:11:38,823
recall ninety. Detector B: precision ninety,

259
00:11:38,823 --> 00:11:40,190
recall forty-five.

260
00:11:40,540 --> 00:11:43,951
Each salesperson quoted the number that made them look good.

261
00:11:43,951 --> 00:11:47,370
Neither was lying. They just answered different questions.

262
00:11:48,370 --> 00:11:51,910
In fact, this is the same swap we saw in the prosecutor's

263
00:11:51,910 --> 00:11:55,006
fallacy. How often does an alarm mean fraud,

264
00:11:55,006 --> 00:11:57,660
versus how often does fraud cause an alarm.

265
00:11:58,010 --> 00:11:59,283
Now here's the key idea.

266
00:11:59,283 --> 00:12:02,580
A and B are often the same model, with the dial set differently.

267
00:12:02,930 --> 00:12:05,629
Most detectors give each payment a score,

268
00:12:05,629 --> 00:12:09,541
say from zero to one, for how suspicious it looks.

269
00:12:09,541 --> 00:12:11,248
Then you pick a cut-off.

270
00:12:11,248 --> 00:12:14,360
Above it, alarm. Below it, quiet.

271
00:12:14,710 --> 00:12:18,576
Set the cut-off high, and only the most suspicious payments get

272
00:12:18,576 --> 00:12:21,515
flagged. Fewer false alarms, more misses.

273
00:12:21,515 --> 00:12:23,551
High precision, low recall.

274
00:12:23,551 --> 00:12:24,910
That's Detector B.

275
00:12:25,260 --> 00:12:28,307
Set it low, and you flag lots of payments.

276
00:12:28,307 --> 00:12:30,572
More catches, more false alarms.

277
00:12:30,572 --> 00:12:32,837
High recall, low precision.

278
00:12:32,837 --> 00:12:34,360
That's Detector A.

279
00:12:34,710 --> 00:12:38,222
Push it even lower, and you get a nervous Detector C.

280
00:12:38,222 --> 00:12:41,294
It flags four hundred payments and catches ninety-nine of the

281
00:12:41,294 --> 00:12:42,090
hundred frauds.

282
00:12:42,440 --> 00:12:44,020
Ninety-nine percent recall.

283
00:12:44,020 --> 00:12:47,130
But only ninety-nine of its four hundred alarms are real.

284
00:12:47,130 --> 00:12:49,050
Precision is about one in four.

285
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:52,655
And its accuracy drops to about ninety-seven percent,

286
00:12:52,655 --> 00:12:55,375
lower than the lazy detector that catches nothing.

287
00:12:55,375 --> 00:12:57,380
Accuracy is pointing the wrong way.

288
00:12:58,580 --> 00:13:01,129
That's the trade-off. Move the dial one way,

289
00:13:01,129 --> 00:13:03,457
precision goes up and recall goes down.

290
00:13:03,457 --> 00:13:05,162
Move it back, and they swap.

291
00:13:05,162 --> 00:13:07,280
You almost never get both for free.

292
00:13:08,280 --> 00:13:11,781
In a future video, we'll trace this whole dial on one curve,

293
00:13:11,781 --> 00:13:13,429
called the R O C curve.

294
00:13:13,429 --> 00:13:16,130
For now, just remember: the dial exists.

295
00:13:16,730 --> 00:13:18,834
But sometimes you need one number.

296
00:13:18,834 --> 00:13:21,330
To rank ten models, or to put on a report.

297
00:13:21,680 --> 00:13:24,750
The usual compromise is called the F one score.

298
00:13:24,750 --> 00:13:27,820
It blends precision and recall into a single number.

299
00:13:28,170 --> 00:13:30,225
You might think: just average them.

300
00:13:30,225 --> 00:13:34,484
For Detector A, seventy-five and ninety average to eighty-two

301
00:13:34,484 --> 00:13:35,310
and a half.

302
00:13:35,660 --> 00:13:37,839
But a plain average has a problem.

303
00:13:37,839 --> 00:13:40,606
Remember the detector that flags everything?

304
00:13:40,606 --> 00:13:43,200
Recall one hundred, precision one.

305
00:13:43,200 --> 00:13:46,555
The average is fifty point five, which sounds half decent for a

306
00:13:46,555 --> 00:13:47,950
useless detector.

307
00:13:48,750 --> 00:13:52,915
The F one score uses a stricter average, called the harmonic

308
00:13:52,915 --> 00:13:56,470
mean, that gets dragged down hard by whichever number is worse.

309
00:13:56,820 --> 00:13:59,650
Here's the friendliest way to compute it.

310
00:13:59,650 --> 00:14:05,050
F one equals two T P, over two T P plus F P plus F N.

311
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:08,164
In words: count the true positives twice,

312
00:14:08,164 --> 00:14:10,708
then compare them with all the mistakes.

313
00:14:10,708 --> 00:14:13,020
Both kinds of mistake count against you.

314
00:14:13,370 --> 00:14:17,515
For Detector A: two times ninety is one hundred and eighty.

315
00:14:17,515 --> 00:14:19,905
Plus thirty false alarms, plus ten misses,

316
00:14:19,905 --> 00:14:21,490
is two hundred and twenty.

317
00:14:21,840 --> 00:14:25,198
One hundred and eighty over two hundred and twenty.

318
00:14:25,198 --> 00:14:27,940
Nine elevenths. About eighty-two percent.

319
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:32,752
For Detector B: two times forty-five is ninety.

320
00:14:32,752 --> 00:14:34,400
Plus five plus fifty-five.

321
00:14:34,400 --> 00:14:37,478
Ninety over one hundred and fifty.

322
00:14:37,478 --> 00:14:38,650
Sixty percent.

323
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,482
And the flag everything detector?

324
00:14:41,482 --> 00:14:43,964
Its F one is about two percent.

325
00:14:43,964 --> 00:14:46,220
The harmonic mean is not fooled.

326
00:14:47,220 --> 00:14:49,969
So by F one, Detector A wins.

327
00:14:49,969 --> 00:14:54,806
But be careful. F one assumes a miss and a false alarm are

328
00:14:54,806 --> 00:14:59,160
equally bad. And in the real world, they almost never are.

329
00:15:00,160 --> 00:15:02,732
So the real question is not which number is highest.

330
00:15:02,732 --> 00:15:04,580
It's which mistake would you rather make.

331
00:15:04,930 --> 00:15:06,674
Let's put a price on each one.

332
00:15:06,674 --> 00:15:09,590
These are made up numbers, just to show the idea.

333
00:15:09,940 --> 00:15:12,627
Say a missed fraud costs the bank five hundred dollars.

334
00:15:12,627 --> 00:15:14,934
And a false alarm costs about five dollars,

335
00:15:14,934 --> 00:15:17,810
a few minutes of an analyst's time on the phone.

336
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:21,473
Detector A: ten misses times five hundred,

337
00:15:21,473 --> 00:15:23,847
plus thirty false alarms times five.

338
00:15:23,847 --> 00:15:26,940
Five thousand one hundred and fifty dollars.

339
00:15:27,290 --> 00:15:30,625
Detector B: fifty-five misses times five hundred,

340
00:15:30,625 --> 00:15:33,946
plus five times five. Twenty-seven thousand five hundred and

341
00:15:33,946 --> 00:15:35,200
twenty-five dollars.

342
00:15:35,550 --> 00:15:39,215
Nervous Detector C: one miss, plus three hundred and one false

343
00:15:39,215 --> 00:15:41,742
alarms. Only two thousand and five dollars.

344
00:15:41,742 --> 00:15:42,910
The cheapest of all.

345
00:15:43,260 --> 00:15:45,953
And the lazy detector? A hundred misses.

346
00:15:45,953 --> 00:15:47,300
Fifty thousand dollars.

347
00:15:48,300 --> 00:15:51,659
With these prices, the detector with the worst accuracy,

348
00:15:51,659 --> 00:15:55,237
C, is the best choice. Because misses are so expensive,

349
00:15:55,237 --> 00:15:56,430
you want high recall.

350
00:15:57,430 --> 00:16:00,556
But change the prices. Suppose every false alarm freezes a

351
00:16:00,556 --> 00:16:03,682
customer's card at the checkout, and they get so annoyed that it

352
00:16:03,682 --> 00:16:05,900
costs two hundred dollars in lost business.

353
00:16:06,250 --> 00:16:08,778
Now Detector C costs over sixty thousand dollars,

354
00:16:08,778 --> 00:16:11,690
and Detector A wins easily, at eleven thousand.

355
00:16:12,890 --> 00:16:14,348
Same grids. Same counts.

356
00:16:14,348 --> 00:16:18,860
Different answer. The confusion matrix gives you the counts.

357
00:16:18,860 --> 00:16:21,880
Only you can decide what each mistake costs.

358
00:16:23,380 --> 00:16:27,084
Let's practise reading these grids on a few more examples,

359
00:16:27,084 --> 00:16:28,300
each with real counts.

360
00:16:28,650 --> 00:16:32,160
First, an old friend. The screening test from our Bayes video.

361
00:16:32,510 --> 00:16:36,019
One thousand people. One in a hundred has the condition,

362
00:16:36,019 --> 00:16:39,500
so ten are sick and nine hundred and ninety are healthy.

363
00:16:39,850 --> 00:16:42,099
The test catches nine of the ten sick people.

364
00:16:42,099 --> 00:16:45,160
So nine true positives and one false negative.

365
00:16:45,510 --> 00:16:48,208
And it gives a false alarm to one in ten healthy people.

366
00:16:48,208 --> 00:16:50,751
That's ninety-nine false positives, and eight hundred and

367
00:16:50,751 --> 00:16:52,520
ninety-one true negatives.

368
00:16:53,320 --> 00:16:55,212
Recall: nine out of ten.

369
00:16:55,212 --> 00:16:59,040
Ninety percent. The test is good at finding sick people.

370
00:16:59,390 --> 00:17:02,458
Accuracy: nine plus eight hundred and ninety-one,

371
00:17:02,458 --> 00:17:05,000
out of a thousand. Ninety percent again.

372
00:17:05,350 --> 00:17:08,004
But precision. Look down the alarm column.

373
00:17:08,004 --> 00:17:10,859
Nine real cases, and ninety-nine false alarms.

374
00:17:10,859 --> 00:17:12,890
Nine out of one hundred and eight.

375
00:17:13,240 --> 00:17:17,316
That's one in twelve. The exact same answer we found with Bayes'

376
00:17:17,316 --> 00:17:18,050
theorem.

377
00:17:19,250 --> 00:17:20,783
So here's the connection.

378
00:17:20,783 --> 00:17:23,816
Bayes' theorem was secretly a precision calculation.

379
00:17:23,816 --> 00:17:27,290
It asked: given an alarm, how likely is it real?

380
00:17:28,290 --> 00:17:31,754
Now watch what happens if we use the exact same test in a

381
00:17:31,754 --> 00:17:32,840
different crowd.

382
00:17:33,190 --> 00:17:36,512
Say a specialist clinic, where people already have symptoms,

383
00:17:36,512 --> 00:17:38,461
and half of the thousand are sick.

384
00:17:38,461 --> 00:17:40,630
Five hundred sick, five hundred healthy.

385
00:17:40,980 --> 00:17:43,257
The test still catches nine in ten sick people.

386
00:17:43,257 --> 00:17:45,960
Four hundred and fifty caught, fifty missed.

387
00:17:46,310 --> 00:17:49,518
And it still gives false alarms to one in ten healthy people.

388
00:17:49,518 --> 00:17:53,320
Fifty false positives, four hundred and fifty true negatives.

389
00:17:53,670 --> 00:17:55,585
Recall is still ninety percent.

390
00:17:55,585 --> 00:17:58,630
But precision jumps from one in twelve, to four hundred and

391
00:17:58,630 --> 00:18:00,168
fifty out of five hundred.

392
00:18:00,168 --> 00:18:01,330
Ninety percent.

393
00:18:02,330 --> 00:18:04,023
Same test. Recall didn't move.

394
00:18:04,023 --> 00:18:07,804
Precision changed completely, just because the crowd changed.

395
00:18:07,804 --> 00:18:11,157
So precision always depends on how common the thing is.

396
00:18:11,157 --> 00:18:14,916
Remember that whenever someone quotes you a precision from a

397
00:18:14,916 --> 00:18:16,180
different setting.

398
00:18:17,380 --> 00:18:20,973
In medicine, recall is usually the priority for a first

399
00:18:20,973 --> 00:18:24,190
screening. Missing a sick person is the costly mistake.

400
00:18:24,540 --> 00:18:28,164
Low precision is acceptable here, because every positive gets a

401
00:18:28,164 --> 00:18:29,858
second, more careful test.

402
00:18:29,858 --> 00:18:31,690
That's the honest way to use it.

403
00:18:32,290 --> 00:18:35,770
Second example. A spam filter, where the mistakes flip.

404
00:18:36,120 --> 00:18:39,099
One thousand emails. Three hundred are spam,

405
00:18:39,099 --> 00:18:40,800
seven hundred are real.

406
00:18:41,150 --> 00:18:44,450
The filter sends two hundred and eighty emails to the spam

407
00:18:44,450 --> 00:18:47,970
folder. Two hundred and seventy really are spam.

408
00:18:47,970 --> 00:18:49,510
Ten are real emails.

409
00:18:49,860 --> 00:18:53,260
So thirty spam emails reach your inbox, and six hundred and

410
00:18:53,260 --> 00:18:55,600
ninety real emails land safely.

411
00:18:56,400 --> 00:18:59,451
Precision: two hundred and seventy out of two hundred and

412
00:18:59,451 --> 00:19:01,660
eighty. About ninety-six percent.

413
00:19:02,010 --> 00:19:05,228
Recall: two hundred and seventy out of three hundred.

414
00:19:05,228 --> 00:19:06,440
Ninety percent.

415
00:19:06,790 --> 00:19:09,709
Accuracy: nine hundred and sixty out of a thousand.

416
00:19:09,709 --> 00:19:10,920
Ninety-six percent.

417
00:19:11,270 --> 00:19:14,341
And notice, here accuracy is more honest.

418
00:19:14,341 --> 00:19:18,289
Spam isn't rare. A lazy filter that never marks anything as spam

419
00:19:18,289 --> 00:19:21,580
would only score seventy percent, so it can't hide.

420
00:19:22,580 --> 00:19:24,251
Now, which mistake is worse?

421
00:19:24,251 --> 00:19:27,139
A spam email in your inbox is a small annoyance.

422
00:19:27,139 --> 00:19:28,180
You delete it.

423
00:19:28,530 --> 00:19:31,795
But a real email in the spam folder, a job offer,

424
00:19:31,795 --> 00:19:35,280
a bill, a message from your doctor, might never be read.

425
00:19:35,630 --> 00:19:38,518
So for spam, the false alarm is the costly mistake.

426
00:19:38,518 --> 00:19:41,752
You want high precision, even if a few more spam emails get

427
00:19:41,752 --> 00:19:42,160
through.

428
00:19:43,360 --> 00:19:45,135
Same table, same formulas.

429
00:19:45,135 --> 00:19:48,470
But the opposite priority from the medical test.

430
00:19:49,070 --> 00:19:51,070
Now it's your turn. Pause whenever you like.

431
00:19:51,420 --> 00:19:53,570
A lender has two thousand loans.

432
00:19:53,570 --> 00:19:57,409
Two hundred of them will default, meaning the borrower stops

433
00:19:57,409 --> 00:19:57,870
paying.

434
00:19:58,220 --> 00:20:00,984
A model flags two hundred and fifty loans as risky.

435
00:20:00,984 --> 00:20:03,748
One hundred and fifty of them really do default.

436
00:20:03,748 --> 00:20:05,240
One hundred are fine.

437
00:20:05,590 --> 00:20:08,788
Try to fill the rest of the grid, and find the precision and the

438
00:20:08,788 --> 00:20:09,380
recall.

439
00:20:12,380 --> 00:20:16,052
Here's the grid. Two hundred defaults, minus one hundred and

440
00:20:16,052 --> 00:20:18,310
fifty caught, leaves fifty missed.

441
00:20:18,660 --> 00:20:21,988
One thousand eight hundred good loans, minus one hundred false

442
00:20:21,988 --> 00:20:25,730
alarms, leaves one thousand seven hundred true negatives.

443
00:20:26,330 --> 00:20:29,640
Precision: one hundred and fifty out of two hundred and fifty

444
00:20:29,640 --> 00:20:31,110
flagged. Sixty percent.

445
00:20:31,460 --> 00:20:34,896
Recall: one hundred and fifty out of two hundred defaults.

446
00:20:34,896 --> 00:20:36,240
Seventy-five percent.

447
00:20:36,590 --> 00:20:40,548
Accuracy: one hundred and fifty plus one thousand seven hundred,

448
00:20:40,548 --> 00:20:43,450
out of two thousand. Ninety-two and a half percent.

449
00:20:43,800 --> 00:20:47,990
And the F one score: three hundred, over three hundred plus one

450
00:20:47,990 --> 00:20:51,959
hundred plus fifty. Three hundred over four hundred and fifty.

451
00:20:51,959 --> 00:20:52,620
Two thirds.

452
00:20:53,620 --> 00:20:56,551
So, in plain words. When this model says risky,

453
00:20:56,551 --> 00:20:58,423
it's right six times out of ten.

454
00:20:58,423 --> 00:21:01,740
And it spots three out of every four loans that will go bad.

455
00:21:02,740 --> 00:21:06,059
If you got those, you can now read any confusion matrix you'll

456
00:21:06,059 --> 00:21:06,860
ever see.

457
00:21:07,460 --> 00:21:09,510
So where will you actually meet this table?

458
00:21:09,860 --> 00:21:13,552
In machine learning, it's the first thing printed after training

459
00:21:13,552 --> 00:21:17,464
any classifier. Any model that answers yes or no.

460
00:21:17,464 --> 00:21:20,450
Is this a cat? Will this customer cancel?

461
00:21:20,800 --> 00:21:23,872
Most machine learning tools print it with one line of code,

462
00:21:23,872 --> 00:21:27,980
as a grid of four numbers, exactly like the one we built today.

463
00:21:28,330 --> 00:21:30,391
And when the thing you're predicting is rare,

464
00:21:30,391 --> 00:21:33,961
people report precision and recall, never accuracy alone.

465
00:21:33,961 --> 00:21:36,390
Exactly because of the lazy detector.

466
00:21:36,740 --> 00:21:39,513
In data science, it's how you explain a model to people who

467
00:21:39,513 --> 00:21:41,996
don't build models. Of every hundred alarms,

468
00:21:41,996 --> 00:21:44,819
this many are real. Of every hundred cases,

469
00:21:44,819 --> 00:21:45,890
we catch this many.

470
00:21:46,240 --> 00:21:48,665
In finance, it's fraud detection and credit risk,

471
00:21:48,665 --> 00:21:50,870
exactly the two examples we worked through.

472
00:21:50,870 --> 00:21:53,240
And the cut-off is set by the cost of each mistake.

473
00:21:53,590 --> 00:21:57,220
In computer science, it's spam filters, malware scanners,

474
00:21:57,220 --> 00:22:00,410
and systems that watch network traffic for intruders.

475
00:22:00,410 --> 00:22:03,820
Too many false alarms, and people learn to ignore them.

476
00:22:04,170 --> 00:22:07,798
And beyond those four: medical tests, airport security,

477
00:22:07,798 --> 00:22:09,502
factory quality checks.

478
00:22:09,502 --> 00:22:13,318
Anywhere a yes or no decision can be wrong in two different

479
00:22:13,318 --> 00:22:13,750
ways.

480
00:22:14,350 --> 00:22:17,150
Let's go through the mistakes beginners make most often.

481
00:22:17,500 --> 00:22:21,019
Mistake one: trusting accuracy when the class is rare.

482
00:22:21,019 --> 00:22:24,729
Always ask how rare it is, and compare with the lazy model that

483
00:22:24,729 --> 00:22:25,770
always says no.

484
00:22:26,570 --> 00:22:30,681
For our fraud data, that lazy baseline is ninety-nine percent.

485
00:22:30,681 --> 00:22:34,149
So Detector A's ninety-nine point six only beats doing nothing

486
00:22:34,149 --> 00:22:37,852
by a sliver. The grid shows what that sliver is worth: ninety

487
00:22:37,852 --> 00:22:38,480
frauds caught.

488
00:22:39,080 --> 00:22:42,483
Mistake two: mixing up precision and recall.

489
00:22:42,483 --> 00:22:45,885
Here's the fix. Precision starts from the alarms.

490
00:22:45,885 --> 00:22:48,080
Recall starts from the real cases.

491
00:22:48,430 --> 00:22:52,054
Ask yourself: am I dividing by everything the model flagged,

492
00:22:52,054 --> 00:22:54,220
or by everything that was really there?

493
00:22:55,020 --> 00:22:58,274
Here's a second check. Precision's bottom number is the size of

494
00:22:58,274 --> 00:23:00,856
the alarm column, one hundred and twenty.

495
00:23:00,856 --> 00:23:03,396
Recall's bottom number is the size of the fraud row,

496
00:23:03,396 --> 00:23:04,330
one hundred.

497
00:23:04,930 --> 00:23:09,399
Mistake three: assuming every table is laid out the same way.

498
00:23:09,399 --> 00:23:13,112
Some tools put the truth in the rows, some in the columns.

499
00:23:13,112 --> 00:23:16,070
Some put the positive class first, some last.

500
00:23:16,420 --> 00:23:20,016
So before you read any number, read the labels.

501
00:23:20,016 --> 00:23:23,150
Find the alarm column and the real fraud row first.

502
00:23:23,950 --> 00:23:27,496
If you're ever unsure, find the box with the true positives

503
00:23:27,496 --> 00:23:30,027
first. Then ask: which way does its row go,

504
00:23:30,027 --> 00:23:31,910
and which way does its column go?

505
00:23:32,510 --> 00:23:36,270
And a bonus mistake: judging a model by one number.

506
00:23:36,270 --> 00:23:40,237
Any single score, even F one, hides which mistake the model

507
00:23:40,237 --> 00:23:42,130
makes. Look at the whole grid.

508
00:23:43,130 --> 00:23:45,140
Here's the whole method as a recipe.

509
00:23:45,490 --> 00:23:47,354
Step one: draw the grid.

510
00:23:47,354 --> 00:23:51,118
Truth in the rows, the detector's answer in the columns.

511
00:23:51,118 --> 00:23:52,050
Label them.

512
00:23:52,400 --> 00:23:54,644
Step two: fill the four boxes with counts,

513
00:23:54,644 --> 00:23:57,270
and check that the rows add up to the real totals.

514
00:23:57,620 --> 00:24:01,411
Step three: for precision, read down the alarm column.

515
00:24:01,411 --> 00:24:04,500
Of everything flagged, how much was real?

516
00:24:04,850 --> 00:24:08,820
Step four: for recall, read across the real row.

517
00:24:08,820 --> 00:24:12,570
Of everything real, how much did we catch?

518
00:24:12,920 --> 00:24:15,210
Step five: decide which mistake costs more,

519
00:24:15,210 --> 00:24:16,869
and choose your cut-off to match.

520
00:24:16,869 --> 00:24:20,790
Use accuracy only when the groups are roughly balanced.

521
00:24:21,590 --> 00:24:25,563
Try it on Detector A. The grid: ninety, ten,

522
00:24:25,563 --> 00:24:28,858
thirty, nine thousand eight hundred and seventy.

523
00:24:28,858 --> 00:24:30,726
Precision: three in four.

524
00:24:30,726 --> 00:24:34,003
Recall: nine in ten. And if misses cost more,

525
00:24:34,003 --> 00:24:36,840
pick the detector with the higher recall.

526
00:24:37,840 --> 00:24:40,959
Because a detector is not good or bad on its own.

527
00:24:40,959 --> 00:24:43,870
It's good or bad at the mistakes you can afford.

528
00:24:45,370 --> 00:24:48,944
Look around your world, and notice how many things quietly

529
00:24:48,944 --> 00:24:50,840
answer yes or no for you.

530
00:24:50,840 --> 00:24:54,375
Your inbox, your bank card, your phone's face unlock.

531
00:24:54,375 --> 00:24:57,230
Each one is making both kinds of mistake.

532
00:24:57,830 --> 00:24:59,702
If this lesson made things clear for you,

533
00:24:59,702 --> 00:25:02,309
please subscribe to Math Lab TV, so we can keep building

534
00:25:02,309 --> 00:25:03,080
together.

535
00:25:03,880 --> 00:25:05,430
But here's a puzzle for next time.

536
00:25:05,780 --> 00:25:09,326
Our spam filter was right ninety-six times out of a hundred.

537
00:25:09,326 --> 00:25:11,723
But how does it decide, in the first place,

538
00:25:11,723 --> 00:25:14,315
that an email full of words like free, winner,

539
00:25:14,315 --> 00:25:16,100
and urgent is probably spam?

540
00:25:16,450 --> 00:25:20,615
It turns out it's Bayes' theorem again, applied one word at a

541
00:25:20,615 --> 00:25:23,987
time. Next time, we'll build a spam filter from scratch,

542
00:25:23,987 --> 00:25:25,440
and see how it works.

543
00:25:25,790 --> 00:25:27,410
I will see you in the next video.
